              <h3>Interview with professionals/key workers</h3>
<p>Please see the following interview video of professionals' responses to the following question:</p>
<p>In what circumstances, do you think, confidential information should be disclosed to others?</p>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="ua_u2_s1_p2_interview_disclosing_info" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="ua_u2_s1_p2_interview_disclosing_info undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/ua_u2_s1_p2_interview_disclosing_info.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/ua_u2_s1_p2_interview_disclosing_info.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p>Zoe: Circumstances in which it can sometimes be important to disclose confidential information is when there's a safety issue. So when sharing information is in the best interest of the safety of the child or young person. Even then, it's important to explain to the child, or young person that you plan to share that information.</p>
<p>Ellen: When it comes to safeguarding concerns, we have to protect that young person. Say if they came to us with really serious information, like they might say &quot;such and such has happened at home&quot;. You know, they might say to us, &quot;oh please, can you not tell anyone?&quot; But we can't promise that we have to say, &quot;look, we don't want to, but, to protect you we might have to tell our safeguarding lead&quot;.</p>
<p>Phil: It was discovered that there was a safeguarding issue then obviously that would need to be shared with the designated safeguarding lead and the appropriate step following a safeguarding policy, basically, but that's if there was a need and which is why, again, you can't promise a young person that you'll keep something confidential because there may be a safeguarding issue, somebody may be at risk themselves or others, and that information would need to be passed up for safeguarding reasons.</p>
<p>Zoe T: Really it's just, if it's a safeguard in issue, if it's going to cause harm to that person or they're going to cause harm to somebody else. I mean, there isn't really any justification for breaching confidentiality under any other circumstances.</p>
<p>Phil: Information to be shared with other staff only if there was a risk  and it needed to be shared. And again, that would probably be staff higher up that that need to know not all staff would need to be aware of every detail.</p>
<p>Kerry: And then it's things like on a need to know basis. It shouldn't just be, for example, chatted about in the staff room. Say medication as an example, not everybody that works here needs to know what medication child A is on, but the people who work with him and those that give that medication halfway through the day.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
